UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 18, Lesson 2: Testing materials in a circuit', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Testing materials in a circuit keeps the battery, lamp and connections the same while swapping only the sample in a gap.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy must samples be about the same size/contact when comparing?\n\nFEEDBACK\nDifferent contact can fake insulator/conductor results; keep contact fair.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTouching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFair-test ten materials. Results table + conclusion listing conductors found. Note any unsure results and why.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Testing materials in a circuit keeps the battery, lamp and connections the same while swapping only the sample in a gap.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and make","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-PQU-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Fair-test ten materials. Results table + conclusion listing conductors found. Note any unsure results and why. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about test linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results.. Correction: Different contact can fake insulator/conductor results; with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses test. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about test, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about test. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for test. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for test. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Different contact can fake insulator/conductor results","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Why must samples be about the same size/contact when comparing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for test; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Different contact can fake insulator/conductor results","Careful use of material and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","Testing materials in a circuit keeps the battery, lamp and connections the same while swapping only the sample in a gap"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with test?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named material and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply test to new material and name the material and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","Name material and explain why it matters only, without linking it to test"],"question":"Which response best applies test with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting"],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, a glass marble, a fallen dry leaf. Which claim about test is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","A named observation plus how it supports the claim"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about test?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about test with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Different contact can fake insulator/conductor results","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Using this science idea — Testing materials in a circuit keeps the battery, lamp and connections the same while swapping only — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about test?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["If the observed feature changes, the claim about test should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true","Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded"],"question":"Which prediction about test can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about test. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses test. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about test linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for test. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about test, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results.. Correction: Different contact can fake insulator/conductor results; with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w18-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise test.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a