→ insulator (for that setup). Dry samples; same press.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy must samples be about the same size/contact when comparing?\n\nFEEDBACK\nDifferent contact can fake insulator/conductor results; keep contact fair.","explain":"Testing materials in a circuit keeps the battery, lamp and connections the same while swapping only the sample in a gap.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Material circuit tests.","caption":"Fingers can fake a path.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Gap test</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">swap sample only</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Fair contact</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">same press</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Gap test: insert material between clips. Lamp on → conductor evidence; off → insulator (for that setup). Dry samples; sa Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Gap test: insert material between clips. Lamp on → conductor evidence; off → insulator (for that setup). Dry samples; sa Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about test with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGap test: insert material between clips. Lamp on → conductor evidence; off → insulator (for that setup). Dry samples; same press. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Fair material compare.","caption":"Ten materials.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Table</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">on/off evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">unequal contact</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about test with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTouching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results. Here is a more productive model: Gap test: insert material between clips. Lamp on → conductor evidence; off → insulator (for that setup). Dry samples; same press.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nDifferent contact can fake insulator/conductor results; keep contact fair.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Touching both clips with your fingers while testing adds your body path and confuses results. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about test with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTesting materials in a circuit keeps the battery, lamp and connections the same while swapping only the sample in a gap. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 18, Lesson 2: Testing materials in a circuit","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Electricity and energy","week":18,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nGap test: insert material between clips. Lamp on → conductor evidence; off → insulator (for that setup). Dry samples; same press.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Material circuit tests.","caption":"Fingers can fake a path.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" 